-# FIELD
-
-[0001] The present disclosure relates to computer-implemented autonomous agent systems, and more particularly to systems in which software agents built on machine-learning models are organized into a management hierarchy that mirrors the staff of an organization (for example, a portfolio manager, program managers, project managers, product managers, and specialist staff), and in which the agents originate, prioritize, dispatch, verify, and score work through closed feedback loops that are recorded in persistent ledgers and that widen or narrow the agents' own scope of autonomous action based on measured outcomes.
-
-# BACKGROUND
-
-[0002] Software agents based on large language models and other machine-learning models can now write code, draft documents, analyze data, and call external tools. In known arrangements, a human chooses each task. In "chat" or "vibe coding" arrangements, a person prompts an agent turn by turn. In agentic engineering tools, an agent multiplies a person's effort, but the person still decides what to build and reviews the result. In cloud coding agents, a person assigns a ticket and continuous-integration checks plus a human reviewer judge the change. Workflow-orchestration systems route events to agents, but the events and workflows are authored by people.
-
-[0003] These arrangements share several technical problems. First, the agent does not decide what work matters next; the bottleneck moves to the human who must choose, prioritize and review every task. Second, there is no closed loop between a decision and its measured result: an agent's change is typically judged by whether tests pass at merge time, not by whether the organizational metric it was meant to move actually moved afterward. Third, autonomy is set statically by configuration (an agent is or is not allowed to merge, deploy, or send), not computed from the record of how reliably that class of action has succeeded. Fourth, when agents are given maintenance responsibility, they tend toward repair-only behavior: attention is allocated to whatever is failing, so functioning products and services receive no improvement at all and the system "devolves into bug fixes." Fifth, the policies that govern agents are static text that does not learn from repeated failures of the agents themselves. Sixth, the same failures recur because nothing groups related failures and assigns their root cause to an accountable party.
-
-[0004] Human organizations solved analogous coordination problems with roles: a portfolio manager allocates capacity across programs by risk and value; program managers own an objective and its metrics; project managers drive work to completion; product managers own a product's continuing improvement; quality staff review; auditors check the books. Known agent systems do not reproduce this division of labor as executable, measured, self-correcting machinery.
-
-[0005] There is therefore a need for a system in which virtual agents perform the managerial functions of an organization (allocation, prioritization, origination, assignment, verification, review, audit and improvement), in which every decision carries a machine-checkable prediction that is later scored, in which the scope of autonomous action is computed from outcomes, and in which improvement of working products is a funded, structural part of the loop rather than a residual of repair.
-
-# SUMMARY
-
-[0006] This summary introduces a selection of concepts that are further described in the detailed description. It is not intended to identify key or essential features, and it does not limit the scope of the claims.
-
-[0007] In one aspect, a computer-implemented system operates an organization through a hierarchy of virtual staff agents. Each virtual staff agent is a persona record that binds a role (for example, portfolio manager, program manager, project manager, product manager, quality reviewer, auditor, or specialist), a charter (an objective, a definition of done, a primary metric, and one or more guard metrics), a decision authority (an enumerated set of actions it may take on shared records), and a model-backed reasoning component. The agents execute on a periodic schedule ("tick"). On each tick, a virtual portfolio manager computes a risk value for each program from the shared records and allocates a bounded number of focus slots across programs, a portion evenly and a portion in proportion to risk, and classifies each program's state (for example constrain, escalate, accelerate, maintain, steady, or idle). Within the slots allocated to it, each virtual program manager ranks candidate work items by their relation to the program's charter (primary-metric items first, then guard-metric items, then severity, then age), writes the selected items into a focus set that an execution queue reads, and takes bounded actions on work items (for example promote, restore, close with machine-read evidence, supersede, demote, or assign a doer), each action being recorded together with its prior value so that it can be reversed.
-
-[0008] In another aspect, the system includes a plus loop that separates the improvement of products from the repair of programs. Products (things a person or customer uses) are recorded in a product register distinct from the program register. Each product receives a fixed share of manager capacity (a "plus budget") regardless of the risk state of its program. For a selected product (for example, the product with the oldest last-improvement time, weighted by usage and open friction), a virtual product manager gathers evidence without requesting input from a human principal, writes a single additive, reversible improvement ("plus") that builds on what exists, states a quantitative prediction before the change, dispatches the change through the same verified execution path as any other work, and after a measurement window keeps the change only if a registered outcome measure improved materially and no guard measure regressed; otherwise the system reverts the change and selects a different angle. Rules prevent degeneration: a ratchet that reverts any plus that lowers a quality guard; a rest period after a set number of consecutive misses; a periodic simplification pass; and a rule that a kept plus seeds the next one ("plus the plus").
-
-[0009] In another aspect, every decision of a virtual agent is recorded with a machine-parsable prediction (for example, "<metric> <operator> <number> within <N> hours"). A scorer compares each prediction with the observed metric at the end of its window and records an outcome. A trust ledger computes, for each pair of surface and action class, a reliability score from recorded outcomes (with a prior), and maps the score to a position on an autonomy ladder (for example observed, assisted, delegated, autonomous, self-directed, self-modifying, self-extending). The position determines what that class of action may do without additional review, so that the autonomy of the system widens and narrows from measured results rather than from static configuration.
-
-[0010] In another aspect, an interrupt gate classifies every proposed action. Only an action that is both irreversible and identity-bound (for example, it commits the principal's money, legal signature, or personal accounts) and that has no engineerable reversible path is routed to the human principal, as a card that carries a default, a reason, and a closing probe that detects when the step is complete. All other actions proceed autonomously, are executed through a canonical path with work claims, review and automatic revert, and are reported as completed.
-
-[0011] In another aspect, the system amends its own governing policy. The system scans its agents' outputs and the principal's messages for recorded signatures (for example, phrases that hand a decision back to the principal, or expressions of repeated frustration). When a signature repeats past a threshold, the system generates a policy amendment proposal, red-teams it, replays it against recent ticks, and adopts it through a reviewed change only if it preserves a set of fixed limits that no amendment may reach. Every version is recorded in a lineage register.
-
-[0012] In another aspect, the system groups work items into families, counts reopenings and new items per family over a window, and when a family recurs past a threshold files a single root-cause item at the front of its program's focus, obtains a diagnosis naming the producer, the closing probe, and the smallest fix, and closes the root-cause item only after the family has been quiet for a set period.
-
-[0013] In another aspect, verification is evidentiary. A unit of work is not complete when its change merges; it is complete when declared live probes pass after deployment. A probe that passed before a deployment and fails after it automatically reverts the deployment. Risky changes are reviewed by models of at least two different model families before they may merge.
-
-[0014] In another aspect, the core (ledgers, ticks, persona records, prediction scoring, trust ledger, interrupt gate and plus loop) is independent of any particular organization, and a tenant configuration supplies the organization's programs, charters, metrics, products, executors and limits, so that the same machinery operates a software company, a marketing or media operation, a finance or operations function, a research laboratory, a nonprofit or public body, or a separated personal plane.
-
-[0015] Further aspects include methods performed by the system and non-transitory computer-readable media storing instructions that cause one or more processors to perform the methods.
