-# DETAILED DESCRIPTION
-
-[0025] The following description sets forth embodiments by way of example. Features of one embodiment may be combined with features of another. Terms such as "may", "can" and "in some embodiments" indicate optional features. Numerical values (intervals, thresholds, caps and weights) are examples; other values may be used. The word "or" is inclusive unless context requires otherwise.
-
-## Definitions
-
-[0026] "Organization" means any entity whose work can be represented as programs, projects, products and work items, including a business, a business unit, a research group, a nonprofit, a government office, or an individual's affairs.
-
-[0027] "Virtual staff agent" or "virtual manager" means a software agent defined by a persona record comprising at least a role, a charter, a decision authority, and a reasoning component, where the reasoning component may include a machine-learning model (for example, a large language model), deterministic rules expressed as database queries, or both.
-
-[0028] "Principal" means the human (or body of humans, such as an owner or board) for whom the organization operates.
-
-[0029] "Program" means a standing objective with a charter that names a primary metric and guard metrics. "Project" means a bounded body of work with a completion state. "Product" means something that a person uses, which has its own usage and quality signals. Programs and products are distinct axes: a product belongs to a program, but its improvement is allocated separately.
-
-[0030] "Work item" (also "issue") means a record of work with at least a title, a program, a priority, a status, and optionally a machine-readable doer line (for example, a line naming a repository, a file and an anchor text where a change is to be made).
-
-[0031] "Tick" means one execution of a scheduled procedure; a tick emits a record of each step it performed.
-
-[0032] "Reversible" means undoable within one cycle without loss of data that is not backed up and without the authority of an external party. "Identity-bound" means committing the principal's legal, financial or reputational identity. "Interrupt-eligible" means both irreversible and identity-bound with no engineerable reversible path.
-
-[0033] "Surface" means a distinct path to an outcome (for example, a specific service, channel, repository or tool). "Action class" means a category of action (for example, merge, deploy, close issue, send message, change configuration).
-
-[0034] "Plus" means a single additive, reversible improvement to a product that builds on what exists, stated with a prediction before it is made. "Plussing" means the continuing loop of selecting, making, verifying, keeping or reverting, and seeding pluses.
-
-## System overview (FIG. 1)
-
-[0035] Referring to FIG. 1, a system 100 serves a principal 102. The principal sets objectives and receives digests but is not the executor of any step in the loop. A governance layer 104 stores the policy that governs all agents (including a set of fixed limits, the interrupt gate, and the self-amending policy described below). A virtual staff hierarchy 106 (FIG. 2) performs managerial functions. A work ledger 108 holds work items, roadmap entries and work claims. A decision and prediction ledger 110 holds each decision with its prediction. An executor pool 112 holds agents and tools that perform work (for example, code-writing agents running on different models, content agents, analysis agents, and, where needed, people). Executors change the managed organization systems 116 (for example, software services, websites, documents, campaigns or operational processes). A verification engine 114 runs declared probes after each change and reverts a change whose probes regress. A signal store 118 holds metrics, usage, failures, friction signals and outcomes. A trust ledger 120 computes trust per surface and action class from outcomes in the signal store and feeds back to the governance layer as scope. The signal store also feeds back to the virtual staff hierarchy, closing the loop.
-
-[0036] In one embodiment the system is implemented on a serverless edge platform: each agent and loop runs as a scheduled worker; shared state lives in a relational database (for example, SQLite-compatible), an object store and a key-value store; database views compute rankings and plans on read; database triggers enforce rules (for example, that every new metric has a trigger row, or that a work item parked for a human is rerouted to the fleet); and the agents' tick procedures are stored as data (workflow definitions) so they can be changed by a reviewed database migration without redeploying code. A human-operated interactive agent session may contribute work, but no step of the loop waits for one. Other embodiments use containers, virtual machines or on-premises servers, and any database.
-
-## Virtual staff hierarchy (FIG. 2)
-
-[0037] Referring to FIG. 2, a virtual portfolio manager 202 allocates capacity across programs. Virtual program managers 204a to 204n each own a program charter. Virtual project managers 206 track milestones and dependencies of projects within a program. Virtual product managers 208 own the product register and the plus loop for their products. Quality and review agents 210 review risky changes; in one embodiment a change is reviewed by models of at least two different model families (for example, from different providers) and must pass both. Specialist staff agents 212 perform functions such as finance, communications, operations, research or legal intake. Executor agents 214 perform the work. An auditor and calibrator agent 216 scores predictions and maintains the trust ledger. Each persona writes a plain-language brief 218 on each tick so that the principal reads one page rather than reviewing individual work items.
-
-[0038] Each persona is stored as a record, for example: `management_personas(persona_id, role, scope, charter_text, decision_authority, path_a)`, together with `program_charters(program, objective, definition_of_done, primary_metric, guard_metrics, manager_persona)` and an agent prompt record. A persona's decision authority enumerates exactly the actions it may take (for example, a program manager may promote, restore, close with evidence, supersede and assign a doer within its program; a portfolio manager may demote certain machine-filed items across programs). A persona may not take actions outside its authority, and a persona serving a separated plane (for example, a personal plane) may not read stores of another plane.
-
-[0039] Personas operate in lanes. In one embodiment, control-plane files and governance files are never originated by managers and change only through a reviewed change with a work claim; a separated personal program never originates roadmap items and never reads research stores.
-
-## Periodic manager tick (FIG. 3)
-
-[0040] Referring to FIG. 3, at step 302 a tick starts on a schedule (for example, hourly). At step 304, the system snapshots, once per tick, a risk value and a quality index for each program into a table, so that later steps read the snapshot rather than re-evaluating expensive views (which in one embodiment was necessary to avoid out-of-memory failures in the database). In one embodiment risk = 3 x (high-priority open items) + 0.3 x (open items) + 15 x (guard-metric breaches) + 2 x (stalled projects), and a verdict is RED when a guard or primary metric is in breach, AMBER when high-priority items or stalled projects exist, and GREEN otherwise. The quality index is the share of measured metric triggers that are on target.
-
-[0041] At step 306, the portfolio manager computes an allocation of a bounded number of focus slots (for example 25) across programs: a first portion evenly and a second portion in proportion to each program's share of total risk. At step 308 it classifies each program: constrain (highest risk), escalate (risk up more than a threshold in 24 hours, or quality index down by a threshold: slots raised, for example by half), accelerate (risk down more than a threshold), maintain (GREEN: a minimal allocation), steady, or idle (no open work). Each allocation is recorded per program per tick.
-
-[0042] At step 310, each program manager ranks its candidate items in tiers: tier 0, items tied to the charter's primary metric; tier 1, items tied to a guard metric; tier 2, critical; tier 3, high; tier 4, other; then by priority and age. The top items up to the program's slot allocation form its focus set. The program manager writes a priority score for each focus item into a scoring table read by the execution queue, so that the execution agents take focus items ahead of others of their tier; when an item leaves focus, the manager removes only the score rows it wrote itself.
-
-[0043] At step 312, the managers act on items within their authority, each action being written to an actions table with its prior value: promote (raise a focus item's priority) and restore (return it on leaving focus); close (an item whose metric reading has returned to target on a fresh reading, past a minimum age and with no failing contract, closes with that reading as evidence; the metric's own trigger re-files it if it breaches again); supersede (an older duplicate closes in favor of a newer one); demote (a stale critical item filed by a machine loop, with no doer and no session, becomes high; items filed by the principal or a session are never demoted); assign a doer (for a focus item with no doer for a period, the manager asks a model-backed agent for one target and anchor, validates the answer against lane rules, writes it into the item so an executor can build it, or records a no-fix finding with its reason so that a gap is never silent).
-
-[0044] At step 314, managers originate work rather than only repairing. Once per period, each program manager with fewer than a threshold of open roadmap items asks a research agent to propose one new feature or quality improvement from the program's aim, quality index, worsening metrics and the relevant code or documents; an answer in a prescribed form (title, rationale, target, anchor, edit) becomes a roadmap proposal and, after validation, a work item that the execution path builds, merges on green checks, deploys with canary and revert, and verifies live. A daily cap bounds origination. Plus-loop pluses (FIG. 4) are also originated in this step.
-
-[0045] At step 316, the tick logs a decision record with a parsed prediction whenever the allocation changes, writes a brief per persona, and updates metrics such as items in focus closed in 24 hours and focus items without a doer (each with its own trigger). Switches stored as configuration rows stop the actions without a redeploy while measurement continues.
-
-## Plus loop (FIG. 4)
-
-[0046] Repair-weighted allocation alone starves functioning products: a product in a healthy program receives no focus. The plus loop corrects this structurally. Products are recorded in a product register (for example `product_registry(product, program, cadence, usage_value, usage_window, usage_source, quality_signal, last_plus_at)`) distinct from the program register, and each product receives a fixed plus budget that is not taken from repair capacity on a leftover basis; in one embodiment, reducing it requires a configuration row with an expiry.
-
-[0047] Referring to FIG. 4, at step 402 the product manager picks a product, for example the one with the oldest last-plus time, weighted by usage and open friction, with at most one plus in flight per product and a fleet-wide daily cap. At step 404 it gathers evidence without asking the principal: usage by section (what is never opened), empty and error states, failing probes, slow routes, reader or user feedback, the principal's earlier requests and owed items, and features of comparable best-in-class products that this one lacks. A product with no usage signal receives a measurement plus first. At step 406 it writes one plus: the smallest reversible addition that builds on what exists (never a removal and never a rewrite), with a prediction (metric, direction, size) stated before the change. At step 408 the plus is filed as an ordinary work item with a doer line, so the same execution, review, merge, canary and automatic revert apply; no separate trust path is created.
-
-[0048] At step 410, at verified deployment the system captures a baseline of a registered outcome measure and a separate output-quality guard measure, and after a window (for example seven days) re-reads both. A missing or stale measure counts as unmeasured, never as a pass. At step 412, if the outcome improved materially and no guard regressed, the plus is kept and seeds the next plus for the same product ("what did this make possible?"). At step 414, otherwise the system requests a bounded revert of that addition and selects a different angle; after a set number of consecutive misses (for example three) the product rests for a period and is then reviewed for a fresh angle (a different user, a different surface). At step 416, every Nth cycle for a product (for example every fifth) is a simplification pass, because additions alone grow complexity, and a weekly digest of what got better is written for the principal instead of a notification per change. A plus never becomes a question to the principal.
-
-[0049] Counter-measures are part of the loop: against metric gaming, the quality signal is a separate outcome measure and a plus is judged on both; against feature creep, the ratchet and the simplification pass; against starvation under high risk, the fixed share; against absence of data, the measurement plus.
-
-[0050] The plus loop is a "yes, and" discipline: review of existing work arrives as a better version rather than as a verdict, so the organization improves continuously instead of only being maintained.
-
-## Predict, act, verify and score (FIG. 5)
-
-[0051] Referring to FIG. 5, at step 502 every decision is recorded with a prediction in a parsable form, for example "<metric> <operator> <number> within <N> h", or a seeded structured prediction. At step 504 the decision is executed through a canonical path: a work claim prevents two agents from changing the same thing; the change is made; risky changes pass a two-family review gate. At step 506, after deployment, the declared probes of the affected component run. If a probe that passed before fails after, at step 508 the system automatically reverts and records the failure. Otherwise, at step 510, the work item closes only with machine-read evidence that the change is live and effective; a merge alone is not completion. At step 512, at the end of the prediction window, a scorer compares the observed metric to the prediction and records an outcome. At step 514 calibration statistics (for example, the share of predictions matched, by persona, action class and surface) and the trust ledger are updated. Metrics such as the share of decisions logged with a prediction, the prediction miss rate, and predictions left unscored beyond a deadline are themselves registered with triggers.
-
-## Trust-gated autonomy and the interrupt gate (FIG. 6)
-
-[0052] Referring to FIG. 6, at step 602 an agent proposes an action. At step 604 the interrupt gate determines whether the action is irreversible and identity-bound with no reversible path. The system first attempts to engineer reversibility (for example, by taking a backup, staging a draft, or using a canary). If the action remains interrupt-eligible, at step 606 the system writes a card for the principal that carries an identity-bound line, the default that is in effect until the principal acts, the reason, and a closing probe that detects completion; a card without such a line is automatically rerouted to the fleet. Such cards are limited to steps only the principal can take, such as minting a credential, spending money, signing, or acting in the principal's own accounts.
-
-[0053] Otherwise, at step 608, the system reads the trust ledger for the action's surface and action class. Trust may be computed as reliability with a prior (for example (successes + a) / (attempts + a + b), with a baseline of 0.5), optionally decaying when unexercised and optionally weighted by event type (for example, rising on verified autonomous action, clean self-remediation, or closure of a friction signal, and falling on a silent drop of a work item, a hidden failure, an unnecessary question to the principal, or a handoff of an executable step to the principal). If trust is below the threshold for the needed rung, at step 610 the system narrows: it uses an assisted path or adds review. Otherwise, at step 612 it executes autonomously within the rung's scope. At step 614 the outcome adjusts trust and any rung transition is recorded. In one embodiment, rising trust widens only the scope of the agents' own loops and only through the canonical reviewed path; no rung can reach the platform's permission checks, secrets, the interrupt gate, or the fixed alignment limits.
-
-[0054] Agents are monitored for "false stops": outputs that pause, ask, offer a menu, or assign the next step to the principal when the action was not interrupt-eligible. The system records recognized signatures in a register, scans agent outputs hourly, excludes outputs that plainly state a refusal or limit with its reason, and counts the hits as a metric with a target of zero. This makes the agents act as accountable staff rather than as assistants waiting for instruction.
-
-## Self-amending policy (FIG. 7)
-
-[0055] Referring to FIG. 7, at step 702 the system scans agent outputs and principal messages for signatures recorded in registers (for example, conditional offers, report-as-question, a menu of options, an unaccepted blocker, friction phrases from the principal such as an expression that the principal should not have to repeat an instruction). At step 704, when a signature repeats past a threshold (for example three times in seven days from one source), or any friction phrase appears, at step 706 a policy amendment proposal is generated. At step 708 the proposal is red-teamed and replayed against trailing ticks. At step 710, if it preserves the fixed limits and improves the replay, at step 712 it is adopted through a reviewed change, the policy version is bumped, and the version and its source are recorded in a lineage register; otherwise at step 714 it is rejected with its reason. A periodic audit work item reviews open proposals. Classifier or permission refusals are inputs to this process: if the condition is in the system's own text, the text is fixed (for example, by writing a rule's real scope into the rule) and the action is attempted once more; a refusal that stands is stated plainly and the next path is taken, never a rewording to pass the same check.
-
-## Recurrence and root cause (FIG. 8)
-
-[0056] Referring to FIG. 8, at step 802 the system groups work items into families (for example by title prefix, or per metric trigger) and counts reopenings and new items in the last 7 days and 24 hours. At step 804 a family reopened at least K times (for example 5) and still active triggers, at step 806, a single root-cause item at top priority, which places it in its program's focus at the front of the execution queue and on the roadmap. At step 808 a diagnostic agent names the producer of the failures, the closing probe and the smallest fix, appended to the item. At step 810 the root-cause item closes with evidence once the family has had no reopening and no new item for a period T (for example 24 hours), and reopens automatically on recurrence. A per-program quality trend is recorded each tick, and a program whose quality index falls by a threshold is escalated by the portfolio manager exactly as a risk rise is.
-
-[0057] Also part of the loop is an issue accounting rule: every work item that leaves the active set must carry a disposition (done with evidence, remediated, or backlogged with a trigger); an item that leaves without one is a "silent drop", recorded as an incident.
-
-## Additional registers
-
-[0058] Embodiments maintain some or all of: a failure-mode register, each failure mode having a detector query run periodically; a single-point-of-failure register; a belief register whose entries carry probes that can falsify them; a capability ledger; a blocker red-team register (a claim that work cannot proceed is accepted only after all surfaces are enumerated and tested); an objective space (ratified objectives, derived goals, and probationary novel objectives); an inferred-direction register (categories the principal acted on repeatedly in a window, read by the execution queue as a priority factor); a vocabulary register of defined terms; and a metric-authorship register in which each new metric is on probation until it holds a value.
-
-[0059] In one embodiment the execution queue computes priority on read as a function of impact, probability, gains, cost, trust for the relevant surface and action class, and inferred direction, and records each pull.
-
-## Tick evidence
-
-[0060] Each loop records the steps it performed per time slot (for example: reconcile; recognize and redirect; update inferred direction; pull from queue; dispatch or execute; verify; compare prediction; log provenance; self-adoption gate; report; accrue trust; probe failure modes; scan capability gaps; and evolve policy), and a metric records the share of slots whose steps completed. Reports to the principal are short and declarative: what happened, what was decided, what is next, and what remains open.
-
-## Full autonomy as earned authority
-
-[0059a] In the embodiments, "full autonomy" means that the virtual staff, not the principal, are the actor of every ordinary step across the whole organization: they find the work, decide, execute, verify, report and improve, with purview over every program, project and product in the tenant configuration. Autonomy is not granted by a static permission setting. It is earned and computed, in the following way.
-
-[0059b] Act first, then verify. For any action that is reversible, the agent executes in the same cycle in which it reaches the conclusion, and does not ask the principal. A conclusion that would otherwise be returned as a question, an offer or a menu is instead adopted with its default, executed and reported as done (the "self-adoption gate"). Verification, not prior permission, is the control: live probes, automatic revert and scored predictions.
-
-[0059c] Reversibility is engineered before an action is classified. Before an action is treated as irreversible, the system attempts to construct a reversible path, for example by taking and checking a backup, staging the change behind a flag, deploying to a canary, writing a draft instead of sending, or splitting the action into a reversible part and a small irreversible part. Only what remains irreversible after this step is considered by the interrupt gate, so the gate is narrow by construction.
-
-[0059d] Scope widens with verified outcomes. Each verified autonomous outcome raises trust for its surface and action class, and rung transitions on the autonomy ladder are automatic and recorded. At the higher rungs the agents change their own loops and rules ("self-modifying") and create new capabilities and loops ("self-extending"), always through the canonical reviewed change path (pull request or database migration, with canary and revert), so that a self-change is itself verified and reversible.
-
-[0059e] The only pause. The interrupt gate pauses an action only when it is both irreversible and identity-bound with no engineerable reversible path (for example, spending the principal's money, signing a legal document, or destroying data that has no backup). Even then the system does not stop: it writes a principal card with the default in effect and a closing probe, and continues all other work. An embodiment measures false stops (pauses that were not interrupt-eligible) with a target of zero, so that over-caution is treated as a defect just as an error is.
-
-[0059f] Heartbeat with evidence. The system runs as a continuous heartbeat: a scheduled tick executed by at least two independent producers so that one failure does not stop it, each tick recording, per time slot, the steps it executed and their results (reconcile, recognize and redirect, update inferred direction, pull from the queue, dispatch or execute, verify, compare prediction, log provenance, self-adoption gate, report, accrue trust, probe failure modes, scan capability gaps, evolve policy). A slot with no record is itself a detected failure. Because the heartbeat runs on the platform and not in a human-operated session, the organization operates when no person or chat session is present.
-
-## Organization kit
-
-[0059g] The core is delivered as a reusable organization kit in which only roles and data vary between organizations. The kit comprises: persona templates for each role; charter templates; the ledgers and their views (work, claims, decisions and predictions, outcomes, trust, failure modes, products and pluses, lineage); the tick procedures stored as data; the default policy pack; the probe and revert path; and the principal queue. Installing the kit for a new organization consists of supplying a tenant configuration (programs, charters and metrics, products, executors, model ladder, hosts, principal contact, separated planes) and connecting its systems as surfaces with probes. No code of the core changes between tenants. A boundary check fails if any component is not classified as core, application or personal.
-
-## Further principles applicable to any autonomous platform
-
-[0060a] Work claims and leases. Before an agent (virtual or human-operated) changes a component, it acquires a claim in a shared claim ledger naming the component, the claimant and an expiry; a second agent that finds a live claim does not duplicate the work and may instead review the claimed change. A periodic reaper releases expired claims. Claims may carry a fencing token so that a stale holder's writes are rejected. Deployment of a component holds a lock and appends to a deploy ledger.
-
-[0060b] Reconciliation with no silent drops. On every tick a reconciler compares the set of work items in each state with the previous tick and with the records of each loop that consumes them, and requires that every item that left the active set carries a disposition (done with evidence, remediated, or backlogged with a trigger and a closing probe). An item that disappears without one is a silent drop, recorded as an incident and reopened.
-
-[0060c] Principal action queue with defaults. Steps that only the principal can take are kept in a single queue, each as a card with an identity-bound line, the default in effect until the principal acts, the reason and a closing probe that closes the card automatically when the step is observed done; cards for anything else are rerouted to the agents. The principal therefore never acts as the executor of an ordinary step, and the queue is measured (for example, cards open without an identity-bound line, target zero).
-
-[0060d] Kill switches as data. Every loop reads an on/off configuration row and stops its actions, but not its measurement, when the row is off, so any behavior can be stopped without a deploy; drills periodically confirm the switches work.
-
-[0060e] Blocker red-teaming. A claim by an agent that work cannot proceed is accepted only after the distinct surfaces that could reach the same outcome have been enumerated and tested, each test recorded; an untested blocker counts against trust.
-
-[0060f] Capability advertising. Each component publishes a machine-readable description of what it can do, its probes and its owner, so that managers can find a doer for a work item by reading the descriptions rather than by human knowledge of the system.
-
-[0060g] Cost-aware model ladders. Each task class has an ordered list of models by cost and capability; a breached spending cap moves tasks down the ladder rather than stopping work, and cost per verified outcome is recorded.
-
-[0060h] Separated planes. Data and agents of distinct planes (for example, a personal plane and an organizational plane) are isolated so that an agent of one plane cannot read stores of another, enforced by authorization of the requester rather than by prompt text alone.
-
-## Application to any organization (FIG. 9)
-
-[0061] Referring to FIG. 9, an organization-independent core 902 comprises the ledgers, the tick machinery, the persona records, prediction scoring, the trust ledger, the interrupt gate and the plus loop. A tenant configuration 904 supplies the organization's programs, charters, primary and guard metrics, products, executors, model ladder, hosts, principal contact and limits. In one embodiment a boundary file classifies each component as core, application or personal, and a check fails when a component is unclassified. A default policy pack is supplied, with the organization's own directives as an overlay.
-
-[0062] Examples: in a software company 906a, programs are operations, infrastructure, growth and research; products are services and applications; executors are coding agents; probes are health checks. In a marketing or media operation 906b, programs are channels and audiences; products are a newsletter, a video channel or a website; a plus is one new section or format, judged on engagement with an unsubscribe or complaint guard; outreach is subject to consent, suppression and opt-out rules that no rung can relax. In an operations or finance function 906c, programs are processes (for example, close, collections, procurement); products are reports and dashboards used by staff; probes are reconciliations; spending is interrupt-eligible. In a research laboratory 906d, programs are research lines; products are papers, datasets and demos; a plus is an added figure, an errata response or an explainer. In a nonprofit or public body 906e, programs are service programs and grants; products are public services and pages. In a personal or family office 906f, a separated plane runs its own managers that never read the organization's stores.
-
-[0063] Mixed human and virtual staffing is supported: any persona may be filled by a person, in which case the same records, predictions, briefs and trust accounting apply to that person's decisions.
-
-## Example implementation and results
-
-[0064] In one implementation (the "customer zero" installation), the system ran as approximately 39 scheduled workers on a serverless edge platform with a relational database, object store and key-value store; one virtual portfolio manager and five virtual program managers (operations, infrastructure, reach, research and personal) ran an hourly tick. Over the observation period, more than 6,600 work items passed through the work ledger; more than 3,800 changes were merged into the main repository, of which more than 600 were opened by the system's own code loop from items it filed and others by third-party coding agents coordinated through a single work-claim ledger; more than 1,000 decision predictions were scored, of which about 73% matched; more than 600 failure modes had detectors; and trust was scored per surface and action class. In a seven-day window the managers performed dozens of evidence-based closes, demotions, root-cause filings and lever reviews and originated roadmap items, some of which were deployed and verified live without being requested by the principal. [Practitioner: these figures come from the system's own records as of 2026-11-10; keep or remove.]
-
-## Variations
-
-[0065] The reasoning component of a persona may be any model or combination of models, including models of different families for proposal and review; models may be selected per task from a ladder by cost and capability, and a breached spending cap steers to cheaper models rather than stopping work. Ticks may be event-driven instead of periodic. The ranking tiers, weights, caps, windows and thresholds may be learned from outcomes. Products may be ranked by a bandit algorithm. Ledgers may be distributed or append-only. Briefs may be delivered by page, message or voice. The managed systems may be physical (for example, a facility or fleet of devices) provided that probes measure them.
-
-# CLAIM-STYLE SUMMARIES
-
-[0066] Claims are not required in a provisional application. The following statements describe aspects for which protection may be sought in a later non-provisional application; they are not limiting.
-
-1. A computer-implemented method of operating an organization, comprising: storing, in a database, a plurality of persona records each defining a virtual staff agent with a role selected from a hierarchy comprising at least a portfolio-manager role and a program-manager role, a charter comprising a primary metric and at least one guard metric, and an enumerated decision authority; on each of a plurality of scheduled ticks, (a) computing by the portfolio-manager agent a risk value for each of a plurality of programs from records of open work items and metric readings, (b) allocating a bounded number of focus slots across the programs, a first portion evenly and a second portion in proportion to the risk values, (c) ranking, by each program-manager agent, candidate work items of its program in tiers in which items tied to the primary metric precede items tied to a guard metric, (d) writing the top-ranked items up to the program's allocation into a focus set read by an execution queue that dispatches work items to executor agents, and (e) recording each action taken on a work item together with its prior value and a machine-parsable prediction; deploying a change produced by an executor agent; running declared probes after deployment and automatically reverting the change when a probe that passed before the deployment fails after it; and scoring the prediction against an observed metric value at the end of a prediction window.
-
-2. The method of claim 1, further comprising maintaining a product register distinct from the programs, allocating to each product a fixed improvement budget independent of the risk value of its program, and performing a plus loop comprising selecting a product, gathering evidence without requesting input from a human principal, generating a single additive reversible change with a stated prediction, dispatching it through the execution queue, and after a window keeping the change only if a registered outcome measure improved and no guard measure regressed, and otherwise reverting it.
-
-3. The method of claim 2, wherein a kept change seeds generation of a next change for the same product; a set number of consecutive non-improving changes places the product in a rest state; and every Nth cycle for a product is a simplification change.
-
-4. The method of claim 1, further comprising computing, for each pair of surface and action class, a trust value from scored outcomes, mapping the trust value to a rung of an autonomy ladder, and permitting a proposed action to execute without added review only when the trust value for its surface and action class meets the threshold of the required rung.
-
-5. The method of claim 4, further comprising classifying each proposed action and routing it to a human principal only when it is irreversible and identity-bound and no reversible path can be engineered, the routing comprising a card carrying a default in effect, a reason and a closing probe; and executing all other actions without routing to the principal.
-
-6. The method of claim 1, further comprising grouping work items into families, filing a single root-cause work item at top priority when a family's reopenings in a window exceed a threshold, obtaining from a model-backed agent an identification of a producer, a closing probe and a fix, and closing the root-cause work item only after the family has had no reopening for a quiet period.
-
-7. The method of claim 1, further comprising scanning agent outputs for recorded signatures of deferring an executable step to a human principal, counting matches as a metric, and, when a signature repeats beyond a threshold, generating, red-teaming and replaying a policy amendment and adopting it only if it preserves a fixed set of limits, recording each adopted version in a lineage register.
-
-8. The method of claim 1, wherein the program-manager agent, for a focus item lacking a doer for a period, requests from a model-backed agent a target and anchor for a change, validates the answer against lane rules, and either writes it into the work item for an executor or records a no-fix finding with a reason.
-
-9. The method of claim 1, wherein a work item closes only on machine-read evidence that its change is live and its metric is on target, and wherein a work item leaving an active set without a recorded disposition is recorded as an incident.
-
-10. The method of claim 1, wherein a change is merged only after review by machine-learning models of at least two different model families.
-
-11. The method of claim 1, wherein the persona records, ledgers and tick procedures form an organization-independent core and a tenant configuration supplies the programs, charters, metrics, products, executors and limits of a particular organization.
-
-12. A system comprising one or more processors and memory storing instructions that, when executed, cause the one or more processors to perform the method of any of claims 1 to 11 and 15 to 17.
-
-13. A non-transitory computer-readable medium storing instructions that, when executed by one or more processors, cause performance of the method of any of claims 1 to 11 and 15 to 17.
-
-14. A computer-implemented method of continuously improving a product operated by an organization, comprising the plus loop of claim 2, performed by a virtual product-manager agent, wherein the improvement budget is not reducible without a configuration record carrying an expiry.
-
-15. The method of claim 1, further comprising requiring each agent to acquire, before changing a component, a claim with an expiry in a shared claim ledger, and directing a second agent that finds a live claim to review rather than duplicate the change.
-
-16. The method of claim 1, further comprising reconciling, on each tick, the work items that left an active set against recorded dispositions, and reopening and recording as an incident each item lacking a disposition.
-
-17. The method of claim 5, wherein cards are held in a single principal queue, each card closes automatically when its closing probe observes the step done, and a card lacking an identity-bound line is rerouted to the virtual staff agents.
-
-18. A computer-implemented method of governing autonomous agents, comprising: recording every agent decision with a machine-parsable prediction; scoring each prediction at the end of its window; computing trust per surface and action class from the scores; and widening or narrowing the set of actions each agent may take without review according to the trust, wherein no trust level permits changing platform permission checks, secrets, or the classification rule of the interrupt gate.
-
-19. A computer-implemented method of autonomous operation, comprising: for each conclusion reached by a virtual staff agent, determining whether a resulting action is reversible; when it is not, attempting to construct a reversible path by at least one of taking a verified backup, staging behind a flag, deploying to a canary, drafting instead of sending, or splitting the action; executing every action that is or has been made reversible in the same cycle without requesting approval from a human principal, and verifying it with live probes and automatic revert; routing to the principal only an action that remains irreversible and identity-bound, as a card carrying a default in effect and a closing probe, while continuing all other work; and recording as a defect each pause or question to the principal for an action that was not so routed.
-
-20. The method of claim 19, further comprising raising, from each verified outcome, a trust value for the action's surface and action class and automatically transitioning that pair to a higher rung of an autonomy ladder, wherein at a highest rung the agents modify their own tick procedures and policy and create new loops only through a reviewed, canaried and revertible change.
-
-21. The method of claim 1, wherein the ticks are produced by at least two independent schedulers, each tick records per time slot the steps executed and their results, and a slot lacking a record is detected as a failure.
-
-22. A computer-implemented method of installing an autonomous organization, comprising providing an organization-independent core comprising persona templates, charter templates, ledgers, tick procedures stored as data, a default policy and a probe-and-revert path; receiving a tenant configuration specifying programs, charters, metrics, products, executors and principal contact; connecting the organization's systems as surfaces with declared probes; and operating the method of claim 1 without modifying the core.
-
-# ABSTRACT
-
-A system operates an organization through a hierarchy of virtual staff agents (for example, a virtual portfolio manager, virtual program, project and product managers, reviewers, auditors and specialists), each defined by a persona record with a role, a charter of primary and guard metrics, and an enumerated decision authority. On scheduled ticks the portfolio manager allocates bounded focus capacity across programs by risk, and program managers rank, focus, act on and originate work, recording every action with its prior value and a machine-parsable prediction. Changes are executed through a verified path with live probes and automatic revert, and predictions are scored to compute trust per surface and action class, which widens or narrows autonomy. A plus loop funds evidence-driven, additive, reversible improvement of working products separately from repair, keeping only changes that improve an outcome without regressing a guard. An interrupt gate routes to the human principal only irreversible, identity-bound steps; the system amends its own policy from recurring signatures and resolves recurring failures through root-cause items.
